      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We start by using the tangent addition formula $\\tan(a+b) = \\frac{\\tan a + \\tan b}{1 - \\tan a \\tan b}$. When $a = b = 20^\\circ$, we have:\n\n$$\\tan 40^\\circ = \\frac{\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 20^\\circ} = \\frac{2 \\tan 20^\\circ}{1 - \\tan^2 20^\\circ}.$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\tan 40^\\circ = \\tan(45^\\circ - 10^\\circ)$. By the tangent subtraction formula, we have:\n\n$$\\tan(45^\\circ - 10^\\circ) = \\frac{\\tan 45^\\circ - \\tan 10^\\circ}{1 + \\tan 45^\\circ \\tan 10^\\circ}.$$\n\nSince $\\tan 45^\\circ = 1$, we have:\n\n$$\\tan 45^\\circ - \\tan 10^\\circ = 1 - \\tan 10^\\circ,$$\n$$1 + \\tan 45^\\circ \\tan 10^\\circ = 1 + \\tan 10^\\circ.$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\tan 40^\\circ = \\frac{1 - \\tan 10^\\circ}{1 + \\tan 10^\\circ}.$$\n\nWe are given that $(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) = \\frac{1 - \\tan 10^\\circ}{1 + \\tan 10^\\circ}$. Multiplying both sides by $\\frac{1 + \\tan 10^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 10^\\circ}$, we obtain:\n\n$$(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) \\cdot \\frac{1 + \\tan 10^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 10^\\circ} = 1.$$\n\nThus, $(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) = \\boxed{1}$.",
